[38;5;246m  ⎿  · What does "persist" mean here — and is there a backend outside this repo? → Client-side only, no backend[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Client-side only. Next question.

  [1mThe fork:[0m how long the stored identity survives. This is a stored-format-and-default decision, so it's expensive to
  revisit once other forms depend on it, and it has a real security consequence on shared machines.

  - [1m[38;5;153msessionStorage[0m — cleared when the tab closes. Other forms in the same tab read it fine. Safest default: walking away
    from a shared or public machine doesn't leave the identity behind. Costs you the "remember me across restarts"
    behavior, and it's per-tab, so opening your app in a second tab starts empty.
  - [1m[38;5;153mlocalStorage[0m — survives browser restarts and is shared across tabs. This is what most people mean by "persist," and
    it's what you want if the point is not re-identifying on every visit. The cost is real: on a shared machine the next
    person is silently treated as the previous user until something clears it, and with no backend there's no
    server-side expiry to fall back on. You'd want an explicit logout, which this app doesn't have yet.
  - [1mCookie[0m — same persistence as localStorage but sent to the server on every request. With no backend, you'd be paying
    the cost and getting none of the benefit.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m with an explicit clear/logout function in the same module, because "other forms will
  need it later" strongly implies crossing page loads, which [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m only handles within one tab. But this is a
  judgment call about your deployment: if this ever runs on shared or kiosk machines, [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m is the right
  default and I'd take it instead. Either way the design includes a logout path — without one, [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m has no exit.
